{"page": 1, "text": "Visible text of EX-99.1_2026-10-27_herc2026q3-pressrelease.htm, sha256 16ccc61042dfdcc4264e613fb56716e7bf34c455365e6fbf8302cc686ed64fad.\nEX-99.1 2 herc2026q3-pressrelease.htm EX-99.1\nHerc Holdings Reports Third Quarter 2026 Results Increases 2026 Full Year Guidance\nThird Quarter 2026 Highlights \u2013Record equipment rental revenue of $1,254 million increased 12% \u2013Record total revenues of $1,364 million increased 5% \u2013Net income of $104 million, or $3.10 per diluted share, and adjusted net income of $131 million, or $3.91 per diluted share \u2013Record adjusted EBITDA of $603 million increased 9% with adjusted EBITDA margin up 190 bps\nBonita Springs, Fla., October 27, 2026 -- Herc Holdings Inc. (NYSE: HRI) (\"Herc Holdings\" or the \"Company\") today reported financial results for the quarter ended September 30, 2026. \u201cOur team delivered an exceptional third quarter, leveraging our scale, broad equipment offering, deep customer relationships and superior project execution to capitalize on accelerating customer demand,\u201d said Larry Silber, chief executive officer. \u201cEquipment rental revenue increased 12% on an average OEC fleet that increased less than 2%, resulting in a 360 basis point improvement in dollar utilization. Expert fleet management drove high time utilization and disciplined fleet deployment, while pricing improvement and a higher specialty mix further enhanced fleet efficiency.\n\u201cThe strong performance was led by robust mega-project volume and double-digit expansion in our specialty solutions business,\u201d said Silber. \u201cRevenue synergies from H&E were an increasing contributor in the quarter, consistent with the second-half weighting we previously outlined, as new specialty branches continued to ramp and we advanced cross-selling, broader fleet access and national account penetration. Local-market demand remained stable.\n\u201cGrowth in our large-project pipeline continues to exceed our expectations, with expanding opportunities across data centers, infrastructure, manufacturing facilities, and energy projects. As a result, we are raising our full-year 2026 revenue, adjusted EBITDA and capex guidance for the second time this year,\u201d Silber added. \u201cOur ability to serve these complex projects and deliver for our customers reflects our increased scale and ongoing technological innovation, as well as the hard work, expertise and dedication of Team Herc. I want to thank all of our employees for their continued commitment to safely serving our customers and driving our success.\u201d\n2026 Third Quarter Financial Results \u2022Total revenues increased 5% to $1,364 million compared to $1,304 million in the prior-year period. The increase was driven by a 12% increase in equipment rental revenue resulting from a slightly larger average fleet size, an increase in volume on mega projects and acquisition revenue synergies. Sales of rental equipment decreased by $66 million as disposal volume was elevated in the prior year to optimize the fleet after the H&E acquisition.\n"}
{"page": 2, "text": "\u2022Dollar utilization was 43.5% in the third quarter up from 39.9% in the prior-year period, due to the increase in fleet efficiency and a favorable shift in mix to higher-return fleet on rent year-over-year.\n\u2022Direct operating expenses were $532 million, or 42.4% of equipment rental revenue, compared to $467 million, or 41.6%, in the prior-year period. Operating expenses as a percent of equipment rental revenue were elevated during the period primarily related to increased transportation and fuel expenses as a result of continued macroeconomic headwinds. Higher field incentive compensation, driven by outperformance, especially in fleet efficiency, also contributed to the increase during the quarter.\n1\n\u2022Depreciation of rental equipment increased 4% to $255 million due to higher year-over-year average fleet size. Non-rental depreciation and amortization increased 6% to $74 million primarily due to amortization of acquisition intangibles, particularly the H&E customer relationship intangible asset, and an increase in non-rental asset depreciation resulting from the growth of the business.\n\u2022Selling, general and administrative expenses were $178 million, or 14.2% of equipment rental revenue, compared to $166 million, or 14.8%, in the prior-year period. The decrease as a percent of equipment rental revenue is due to continued focus on improving operating leverage, including acquisition cost synergies, while expanding revenues.\n\u2022Interest expense was $126 million compared with $134 million in the prior-year period, reflecting lower average outstanding borrowings and lower interest rates on floating-rate debt. \u2022Net income was $104 million, or $3.10 per diluted share, compared to $30 million, or $0.90 per diluted share, in the prior-year period. Adjusted net income was $131 million, or $3.91 per diluted share, compared to $74 million, or $2.22 per diluted share, in the prior-year period.\n\u2022Adjusted EBITDA increased 9% to $603 million compared to $551 million in the prior-year period. Adjusted EBITDA margin was 44.2% compared to 42.3% in the prior year period reflecting higher equipment rental revenue, improved operating leverage, partially offset by higher delivery and fuel costs.\nNine Months 2026 Financial Results\n\u2022Total revenues increased 17% to $3,707 million compared to $3,167 million in the prior-year period. This year-over-year increase was driven by a 21% increase in equipment rental revenue resulting from the larger fleet size after the H&E acquisition, an increase in volume on mega projects and revenue synergies. Sales of rental equipment decreased by $29 million during the period as disposal volume was elevated in the prior year to optimize the fleet after the H&E acquisition.\n\u2022Dollar utilization was 39.8%, up from 38.8% in the prior-year period due to the increase in fleet efficiency and the favorable shift in mix to higher-return fleet on rent year-over-year.\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "\u2022Direct operating expenses were $1,476 million, or 44.6% of equipment rental revenue compared to $1,173 million, or 43.0%, in the prior-year period. Operating expenses as a percent of equipment rental revenue were elevated during the period primarily related to the impact of the H&E acquisition including new specialty locations from branch optimization as well as greenfields, all of which take more time to mature. Transportation and fuel costs were also significantly higher year-over-year.\n\u2022Depreciation of rental equipment increased 21% to $739 million due to higher year-over-year average fleet size primarily as a result of the H&E acquisition. Non-rental depreciation and amortization increased 50% to $222 million primarily due to amortization of acquisition intangibles, particularly the H&E customer relationship intangible asset, and an increase in non-rental asset depreciation resulting from the growth of the business.\n\u2022Selling, general and administrative expenses were $479 million, or 14.5% of equipment rental revenue, compared to $411 million, or 15.0%, in the prior-year period. The decrease as a percent of equipment rental revenue primarily was related to continued focus on improving operating leverage, including acquisition cost synergies, while expanding revenues.\n\u2022Interest expense was $380 million compared with $282 million in the prior-year period, reflecting the new debt issued in June 2025 to fund the H&E acquisition, partially offset by lower average interest rates on floating-rate debt year-over-year.\n2\n\u2022Net income was $99 million, or $2.96 per diluted share, compared to $23 million net loss, or $0.75 loss per diluted share, in the prior-year period. Adjusted net income was $186 million, or $5.55 per diluted share, compared to $170 million, or $5.54 per diluted share, in the prior-year period.\n\u2022Adjusted EBITDA increased 18% to $1,538 million compared to $1,299 million in the prior-year period. Adjusted EBITDA margin was 41.5% compared to 41.0% in the prior year period primarily reflecting revenue growth and improved operating leverage, partially offset by higher direct operating expenses, including delivery and fuel costs.\nRental Fleet \u2022Net rental equipment capital expenditures were as follows (in millions):\nNine Months Ended September 30,\n2026 2025\nRental equipment expenditures $ 1,056 $ 835\nProceeds from disposal of rental equipment (319) (306)\nNet rental equipment capital expenditures $ 737 $ 529\n\u2022As of September 30, 2026, the Company's total fleet was approximately $10.0 billion at OEC. \u2022Average fleet at OEC in the third quarter increased 1.7% compared to the prior-year period. \u2022Average fleet age was 46 and 45 months as of September 30, 2026 and 2025, respectively.\nDisciplined Capital Management \u2022The Company opened 2 previously planned greenfield locations in the third quarter.\n"}
{"page": 4, "text": "\u2022Net debt was $8.0 billion as of September 30, 2026, with net leverage of 3.90x compared to $8.2 billion and 3.84x1 in the same prior-year period. Cash and cash equivalents and unused commitments under the ABL Credit Facility contributed to approximately $2.1 billion of liquidity as of September 30, 2026.\n\u2022The Company declared its quarterly dividend of $0.70 and paid to shareholders of record as of September 2, 2026, on September 16, 2026. (1) Net leverage is calculated using pro forma trailing twelve month adjusted EBITDA including the standalone, pre-acquisition results of H&E.\nUpdating 2026 Outlook The Company is raising its full year 2026 equipment rental revenue, adjusted EBITDA, gross and net rental capital expenditures guidance ranges.\nPrevious Guidance Updated 2026 Guidance\nEquipment rental revenue: $4.375 billion to $4.475 billion $4.475 billion to $4.550 billion\nAdjusted EBITDA: $2.05 billion to $2.125 billion $2.10 billion to $2.15 billion\nNet rental equipment capital expenditures: $850 million to $950 million $1.0 billion to $1.1 billion\nGross capex: $1.25 billion to $1.40 billion $1.45 billion to $1.60 billion\nAs a leader in an industry where scale matters, the Company expects to continue to gain share by capturing an outsized portion of the forecasted higher construction spending in 2026, strengthening its fleet through targeted investment and ongoing gains in fleet efficiency, capitalizing on acquisition-related branch optimization and greenfield openings, and cross-selling a diversified product portfolio.\n3\nEarnings Call and Webcast Information Herc Holdings' third quarter 2026 earnings webcast will be held today at 8:30 a.m. U.S. Eastern Time. Interested U.S. parties may call +1-833-461-5787 and international participants should call the country specific dial in numbers listed at https://help.events.q4inc.com/eahc/international-dial-in-numbers, using the meeting ID: 383 873 427. Please dial in at least 10 minutes before the call start time to ensure that you are connected to the call and to register your name and company.\n"}
{"page": 5, "text": "Those who wish to listen to the live conference call and view the accompanying presentation slides should visit the Events and Presentations tab of the Investor Relations section of the Company's website at IR.HercRentals.com. The press release and presentation slides for the call will be posted to this section of the website prior to the call. A replay of the conference call will be available via webcast on the Company website at IR.HercRentals.com, where it will be archived for 12 months after the call. About Herc Holdings Inc. Founded in 1965, Herc Holdings Inc., which operates through its Herc Rentals Inc. subsidiary, is a full-line rental supplier with 615 locations across North America and 2025 total revenues were approximately $4.4 billion. We offer products, services and technologies aimed at helping customers work more efficiently, effectively and safely. Our classic fleet includes aerial, earthmoving, material handling, trucks and trailers, air compressors, and compaction. Our Herc Rentals ProSolutions\u00ae offering includes industry-specific, solutions-based services in tandem with power generation, climate control, remediation and restoration, pumps, and trench shoring equipment as well as our Herc Rentals ProContractor\u00ae professional grade tools. Our ProControl by Herc Rentals\u2122 digital platform combines a seamless e-commerce experience with integrated project and fleet management tools, leveraging telematics and real-time analytics to help customers optimize productivity across their operations. We employ approximately 10,200 employees, who equip our customers and communities to build a brighter future. Learn more at www.HercRentals.com and follow us on Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn. All references to \u201cHerc Holdings\u201d or the \u201cCompany\u201d in this press release refer to Herc Holdings Inc. and its subsidiaries, unless otherwise indicated.\nCertain Additional Information In this release we refer to the following operating measures: \u2022Dollar utilization: calculated by dividing rental revenue (excluding re-rent, delivery, pick-up and other ancillary revenue) by the average OEC of the equipment fleet for the relevant time period, based on the guidelines of the American Rental Association (ARA). \u2022OEC: original equipment cost based on the guidelines of the ARA, which is calculated as the cost of the asset at the time it was first purchased plus additional capitalized refurbishment costs (with the basis of refurbished assets reset at the refurbishment date).\n"}
